---
title: Groups
description: Group related controls with a native `Fieldset`/`Legend`, or replace the container with a `child` snippet and own the group semantics yourself.
---

<script>
	import { Callout } from "$lib/markdown";
</script>

Checkboxes that belong together — terms, toggles, permission sets — need a group name, not just individual labels. Formsnap's `Fieldset` and `Legend` render the native elements and wire the group's description and errors. Both also forward `data-fs-fieldset` and expose `data-fs-error` only while the field has errors.

## Prefer the native fieldset

A native `<fieldset>` names the group through its `<legend>`, and its `disabled` attribute disables every descendant control and excludes them from form submission:

```svelte title="src/routes/agreements/+page.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import { untrack } from "svelte";
	import { superForm } from "sveltekit-superforms";
	import { Control, Fieldset, Label, Legend } from "@emmorts/formsnap";

	let { data } = $props();

	const form = superForm(untrack(() => data.form), { resetForm: false });
	const { form: formData, enhance } = form;

	let groupsDisabled = $state(false);
</script>

<label for="groups-disabled">Disable groups</label>
<input id="groups-disabled" type="checkbox" bind:checked={groupsDisabled} />

<form method="POST" use:enhance>
	<Fieldset {form} name="nativeAccepted" disabled={groupsDisabled} fieldErrors>
		<Legend>Native agreement</Legend>
		<Control>
			{#snippet children({ props })}
				<input type="checkbox" {...props} bind:checked={$formData.nativeAccepted} />
				<Label>Accept native terms</Label>
			{/snippet}
		</Control>
		{#snippet description()}Accept the native terms before saving.{/snippet}
	</Fieldset>

	<button type="submit">Save agreements</button>
</form>
```

## Replace the container with `child`

A `child` snippet replaces the `<fieldset>` element itself. The owned `description`/`fieldErrors` slots cannot be enabled on a full-container replacement, so the snippet renders caller-owned description and error regions with stable IDs and supplies matching `aria-describedby`:

```svelte title="src/routes/agreements/+page.svelte"
<Fieldset {form} name="customAccepted" id="custom-agreement" disabled={groupsDisabled}>
	{#snippet child({ props, errors }: { props: Record<string, unknown>; errors: string[] })}
		<div
			{...props}
			role="group"
			aria-labelledby={`${props.id}-heading`}
			aria-describedby={`${props.id}-description${errors.length ? ` ${props.id}-errors` : ""}`}
			aria-disabled={props.disabled ? "true" : "false"}
		>
			<h2 id={`${props.id}-heading`}>Custom agreement</h2>
			<Control>
				<AgreementControl
					bind:value={$formData.customAccepted}
					disabled={!!props.disabled}
					describedBy={`${props.id}-description${errors.length ? ` ${props.id}-errors` : ""}`}
				/>
			</Control>
			<p id={`${props.id}-description`} data-fs-description="">
				Accept the custom terms before saving.
			</p>
			<ul id={`${props.id}-errors`} data-fs-field-errors="" aria-live="assertive">
				{#each errors as error, index (index)}
					<li data-fs-field-error="">{error}</li>
				{/each}
			</ul>
		</div>
	{/snippet}
</Fieldset>
```

The custom widget uses `useFormControl` for its naming and props. Its visible label takes `control.labelProps.id`, the button references that ID with `aria-labelledby`, and a hidden input transports the boolean — the hidden input is not a second accessible control and does not receive the control's ID or ARIA attributes:

```svelte title="src/routes/agreements/agreement-control.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import { useFormControl } from "@emmorts/formsnap";

	let {
		value = $bindable(false),
		disabled = false,
		describedBy,
	}: {
		value?: boolean;
		disabled?: boolean;
		describedBy: string;
	} = $props();

	const control = useFormControl({});
</script>

<!-- The button is the focus target; its native activation handles both Space and Enter. -->
<span id={control.labelProps.id} data-fs-label="">Accept custom terms</span>
<button
	{...control.props}
	type="button"
	role="checkbox"
	aria-checked={value}
	aria-labelledby={control.labelProps.id}
	aria-describedby={describedBy}
	{disabled}
	onclick={() => (value = !value)}
>
	{value ? "Accepted" : "Not accepted"}
</button>

<!-- Only this input transports the value. -->
<input type="hidden" name={control.props.name} value={value ? "true" : "false"} {disabled} />
```

## How it works

- A replacement container must provide the corresponding semantics itself: an appropriate group role and an accessible name. The example uses `role="group"` with `aria-labelledby` pointing at its visible heading.
- Include the error ID in `aria-describedby` only when the rendered region actually has errors, so server-rendered associations stay accurate.
- One `Control` identifies one accessible control — not its transport input or group wrapper. Spread `control.props` onto the actual focus target.
- The button's native activation handles Space and Enter; JavaScript only updates its checked state.

<Callout type="warning">

`aria-disabled` communicates state but does not disable descendants — an attribute on a `div` is not native fieldset behaviour. Disable the interactive controls and their transport inputs explicitly. And unlike the native checkbox, the role-based widget needs JavaScript to toggle; without it, the hidden input submits its initial value.

</Callout>
